#usda 1.0
(
    defaultPrim = "Tire"
    upAxis = "Y"
    metersPerUnit = 1
)

# A REGOLITH tire — the part of a wheel that meets the ground: what it grips with, and
# what it looks like doing it. Lugged tread, soft compound, tuned for loose lunar soil.
#
# The tire is a separate file because a tire is a separate DECISION. The hub does not
# change when you re-shod a rover, and two rovers with the same wheel can run different
# rubber. A wheel picks one through its `tire` variantSet:
#
#   def Cylinder "Wheel_FL" (
#       prepend references = @lunco://components/mobility/wheel.usda@</Wheel>
#       variants = { string tire = "regolith" }
#   )
#
# HOW IT TRAVELS. `Tire` is a neutral stand-in for the wheel prim that composes it —
# the same idiom `components/mobility/physical_drivetrain.usda` uses. It is an `over`,
# so it defines nothing of its own: it carries OPINIONS (grip, tread, colour) that the
# reference arc path-translates onto whatever wheel pulls it in. The geometry, mass and
# collider those opinions dress are the wheel's, and stay there.
#
# Grip is authored with Omniverse's PhysX vehicle names (`physxVehicleTire:*`), so the
# numbers mean the same thing here as they do in any other PhysX vehicle.

over "Tire" (
    prepend apiSchemas = ["PhysxVehicleTireAPI", "LunCoTireAPI", "PhysicsMaterialAPI"]
    kind = "component"
)
{
    # --- Grip ---
    float physxVehicleTire:longitudinalStiffness = 8000.0 # N per (m/s) of contact slip
    # Tyre↔regolith friction. 1.5, up from 0.8: at 0.8 a 1100 kg rover's cone is
    # ~356 N per wheel against a drivetrain that can ask for far more, so a
    # throttled rover sat in the saturated branch of `update_wheel_spin` —
    # wheels visibly spinning, ~1.3 m/s² of actual thrust, and a long crawl up to
    # the 5 m/s the motor curve allows. The cone is what converts axle torque into
    # travel, so it is the knob that makes a rover *go*, and a deep-lugged tyre
    # biting loose soil is the one place a coefficient above 1 is honest.
    #
    # This does NOT raise top speed: that is ω_max·r ≈ 12.6 rad/s × 0.4 m ≈ 5 m/s,
    # set by `motor.usda`'s no-load speed through the 200:1 in `gearbox.usda`.
    # Grip decides how fast you REACH it (and how much of a slope you keep it on).
    #
    # THE PAIR THAT DECIDES WHEELSPIN is this and `motor.usda`'s `stallTorque`:
    # a wheel slips whenever commanded torque exceeds `μ·N·r`. On a four-wheeler
    # (N ≈ 445 N, r = 0.4) 1.5 gives a 267 N·m cone; on a six-wheeler the same
    # vehicle weight spreads to N ≈ 310 N and the cone is 186 N·m. The motor is
    # sized under the SMALLER of the two — see `motor.usda` — so neither spins on
    # flat ground, and grip is what stops it rather than a coincidence.
    double lunco:tire:frictionCoefficient = 1.5                # tyre↔regolith friction
    # Cornering stiffness — side force per m/s of LATERAL slip, capped at μ·N.
    #
    # A SECOND NUMBER, because they are two mechanisms. The longitudinal term
    # stands in for rolling resistance and drive slip and must stay soft, or a
    # rolling wheel is braked by its own grip; lateral slip is pure sliding and a
    # tire resists it hard. One shared stiffness could only satisfy the softer of
    # the two, and did — at ~50 N/(m/s) a rover met a two-tonne-metre side load
    # with tens of newtons, drifted through every turn, and read as ice.
    #
    # DERIVED FROM WHERE THE TIRE SATURATES, not fitted. With ~445 N on a wheel
    # and μ = 0.8 the cone is ~356 N, so 2000 N/(m/s) reaches it at ~0.18 m/s of
    # lateral slip — about 5° of slip angle at cruise, which is where a lugged
    # tire on soil lets go. The number this replaced, 100, put saturation at
    # 3.6 m/s of side-slip: a tire that never in any manoeuvre reached its own
    # friction limit, and met a hard corner with ~5% of the grip it had.
    #
    # It read as parity only because it was fitted while the joint rover's motor
    # was a 6x velocity source (`lunco:wheel:stallTorqueGain`, since removed).
    # That saturated BOTH realizations' friction cones at all times, so the
    # LINEAR region — the only place this number does anything — was never
    # entered by either. Putting the joint motor back on its authored torque
    # curve dropped both into it, and they disagreed on sight:
    # `drivetrain_parity` swept 52° raycast against 9° physical.
    #
    # Values this stiff used to be unstable (an explicit damper this hard
    # oscillates), which is what drove the original fit downwards. It no longer
    # can: `lunco_mobility::update_wheel_spin` bounds the lateral force by the
    # impulse that exactly arrests the slip, so the term cannot overshoot.
    float physxVehicleTire:lateralStiffness = 12.6

    # --- Contact-solver friction (UsdPhysicsMaterialAPI) ---
    # THE SAME GRIP, STATED IN THE SOLVER'S UNITS. `lunco:tire:frictionCoefficient`
    # above is the tyre's real Coulomb μ and the raycast model uses it directly:
    # it saturates the patch force at exactly μ·N. The PHYSICAL wheel does not get
    # to do that — its cone is negotiated by avian's XPBD contact solver, which
    # under sustained sliding delivers well under the nominal cone (measured on
    # `drivetrain_parity`: a wheel commanded past its traction limit settled at
    # ~0.45 effective against an authored 0.8, i.e. ~56%). Bending
    # `lunco:tire:frictionCoefficient` to compensate would be a lie about the tyre
    # and would silently corrupt the analytic model with it; so the solver gets
    # its OWN authored coefficient, and the ratio between the two is a property of
    # the engine, not of the rubber.
    #
    # It replaces a hard-coded `Friction::new(0.9)` in
    # `lunco_usd_sim::setup_physical_wheel` — but now authored on the tyre it
    # belongs to, so re-shoeing a rover moves BOTH coefficients instead of only
    # the analytic one.
    #
    # 4.45 against the tyre's own 1.5, and the gap is arithmetic, not taste. avian
    # combines this with the ground material (default rule: Average) — against the
    # standard 1.0 ground that is a nominal 2.73 — and delivers ~55% of it under
    # sustained sliding, i.e. an effective ~1.5: the tyre's real μ, which is what
    # the analytic model is using on the other rover.
    #
    # THE RELATION, so this number moves when μ does instead of drifting:
    #   solver = 2·(μ / 0.55) − ground_μ   (ground_μ = 1.0, the standard surface)
    # which reproduces the measured pair below exactly (μ 0.8 → 1.9) and gives
    # μ 1.5 → 4.45. The 0.55 is the solver's delivery under sustained sliding and
    # is the only measured quantity in it; re-sweep `drivetrain_parity` if the
    # substep count or the avian version changes.
    #
    # Swept on `drivetrain_parity` (physical terminal speed against a raycast twin
    # at the same authored μ = 0.8):
    #   0.9  → 1.97 m/s vs 2.13 (7.5% short)
    #   1.9  → 2.16 m/s vs 2.13 (1.4% — the closest measured)
    # The earlier reading that 1.6 changed nothing was taken while the joint motor
    # was a 6x velocity source: it was torque-saturated at every sample, so the
    # rover was sliding on its cone regardless of how big the cone was, and the
    # sweep could not see friction at all.
    #
    # Re-measure if the substep count or the avian version changes — the 55% is a
    # property of the solver, and it is the only thing this number encodes.
    float physics:dynamicFriction = 1.90
    float physics:staticFriction = 1.90

    # --- Tread ---
    # Spin-revealing tread + rim, drawn by the wheel shader. The look is a `Material`
    # under this `over`, so it path-translates onto the wheel with everything else the
    # tire brings: the wheel that composes this tire binds this tire's tread.
    rel material:binding = </Tire/TireLook>
    color3f[] primvars:displayColor = [(0.3, 0.3, 0.3)]

    def Material "TireLook"
    {
        token outputs:surface.connect = </Tire/TireLook/Shader.outputs:surface>

        def Shader "Shader"
        {
            uniform token info:implementationSource = "sourceAsset"
            uniform asset info:wgsl:sourceAsset = @lunco://shaders/wheel.wgsl@
            color3f inputs:rim_color = (0.12, 0.13, 0.14)   # spoke / rim (dark titanium/steel metal)
            color3f inputs:tire_color = (0.05, 0.05, 0.06)  # rubber
            float inputs:spoke_count = 6.0
            float inputs:tread_lugs = 24.0                  # deep, widely-spaced lugs — soil
            float inputs:lug_depth = 0.7                    # full-relief working tread
            float inputs:wear = 0.1                         # near-new
            float inputs:dust_amount = 0.45                 # lives in the soil it grips
            token outputs:surface
        }
    }
}
